#---
# purpose: "two users of one recursive walker read their own return, not the join, when the return is a tuple field the key used to erase"
#---
#
# fz-kdt.199's live reproducer. `G.walk/2` dispatches on its state TAG and on
# its counter; nothing anywhere asks about the payload beside the tag, so
# fz-kdt.183's forwarding rule leaves it FREIGHT and the collapse replaces it
# with an addressed variable: one key, `{:go, list(a0_1_e)}`, for both users.
#
# The payload is also what `walk/2` RETURNS. One activation publishes one
# return, so the one key answered for both users with the join
# `[binary] | [int]`, and `M.strings/1` -- which asks for `[binary]` -- was
# handed that. Deleting the second `dbg` line made the first compile: the whole
# defect in one sentence.
#
# The two clauses that recurse hand the payload on UNCHANGED inside a rebuilt
# tuple, so the position is constant along the ascent and two keys there are
# two users rather than one user's own accumulator ladder.
#
# The `type/no-matching-clause` warning in the golden is this program's own: a
# two-tag state is not exhaustive over every input. Its gutter number is this
# file's, so re-bless (`BLESS=1 cargo test --test fixture_matrix`) after editing
# the prose above.
defmodule M do
  @spec strings([binary]) :: integer
  fn strings(_xs), do: 1
end

defmodule G do
  fnp walk({:stop, acc}, _n), do: acc
  fnp walk({:go, acc}, 0), do: walk({:stop, acc}, 0)
  fnp walk({:go, acc}, n), do: walk({:go, acc}, n - 1)
end

fn main() do
  dbg(M.strings(G.walk({:go, ["x"]}, 2)))
  dbg(G.walk({:go, [9]}, 2))
end
